---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CaseStudyCard from '../components/CaseStudyCard.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
const studies = (await getCollection('caseStudies')).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
const posts = (await getCollection('blog', ({ data }) => !data.draft)).sort(
  (a, b) => b.data.date.valueOf() - a.data.date.valueOf()
);
---
<Base title="Alfred Persson, Senior AI Engineer" description="Senior AI Engineer. I build AI products that prove they work: LLM pipelines, evaluation systems, production guardrails, measured on every change.">
  <header class="hero">
    <div class="hero-grid">
      <div>
        <h1>Alfred<br>Persson</h1>
        <p class="role">Senior AI Engineer</p>
        <p class="hero-headline">I build AI products that prove they work, on every change.</p>
        <p class="hero-scope"><span class="hero-scope__label">End-to-end ownership:</span> user discovery, system design, shipping, and grading the AI's output against real examples.</p>
        <p class="situation">Previously tech lead at ChromaWay, founder of <a href="/case-studies/sveasync/">SveaSync</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-photo-wrap">
        <span class="hero-photo-mark"></span>
        <div class="hero-photo">
          <img src="/assets/alfredpersson.jpg" alt="Alfred Persson" />
        </div>
        <p class="photo-cap">
          <svg class="pin-icon" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M21 10c0 7-9 13-9 13s-9-6-9-13a9 9 0 0 1 18 0z"></path>
            <circle cx="12" cy="10" r="3"></circle>
          </svg>
          Stockholm (early 2027)
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="hero-links">
      <a href="/cv.pdf">CV (PDF)</a>
      <a href="https://github.com/alfredpersson">GitHub</a>
      <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/alfredpersson">LinkedIn</a>
      <a href="mailto:alfredpersson.se@gmail.com">alfredpersson.se@gmail.com</a>
    </p>
  </header>

  <section id="what-i-do">
    <div class="section-label">
      <span class="section-label__num">01</span>
      <h2>What I do</h2>
    </div>
    <p>I build products where AI is the core of the product, or a feature the product has come to depend on.</p>
    <p>What owning that end to end means, concretely:</p>
    <div class="ledger">
      <div class="ledger-row two-layer-row">
        <div class="two-layer">
          <h3>Start from the user's problem.</h3>
          <p>I talk to users to understand what they are trying to get done, and use AI only where it is genuinely the best tool. AI inference is the slowest, most expensive, and least predictable way to solve a problem, so every part of a feature that can be ordinary software should be.</p>
          <p class="technical">In technical terms: Jobs-to-be-done discovery; deterministic logic wherever possible; model calls reserved for the steps that need judgment or language.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="ledger-row two-layer-row">
        <div class="two-layer">
          <h3>Design the right kind of system for the task.</h3>
          <p>Sometimes that is a normal, predictable workflow that calls AI for specific steps. Sometimes it is a system that searches your own data before answering. Sometimes it is an agent that plans and acts on its own. I pick the shape by the task, and keep everything around the AI boring and reliable.</p>
          <p class="technical">In technical terms: Deterministic workflows with LLM steps, RAG pipelines, tool-using agents, human-in-the-loop escalation, multi-tenant isolation.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="ledger-row two-layer-row">
        <div class="two-layer">
          <h3>Agree on what "working" means before building.</h3>
          <p>Before writing code, I collect real example inputs and write down what a correct result looks like for each one. Those examples become automatic checks that run on every change, showing whether each change made quality better or worse.</p>
          <p class="technical">In technical terms: Hand-labeled eval sets with pass or fail criteria, run in CI as the regression suite.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="ledger-row two-layer-row">
        <div class="two-layer">
          <h3>Plan for the model being wrong.</h3>
          <p>A model will be wrong some of the time, so I design that moment on purpose: what the user sees, when the system says it is not sure, and when a human takes over. Every failure that shows up in production becomes another automatic check, so the same mistake cannot return unnoticed.</p>
          <p class="technical">In technical terms: Confidence gates, designed fallback and escalation paths, production errors converted into permanent CI test cases.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="ledger-row two-layer-row">
        <div class="two-layer">
          <h3>Measure whether it actually helps.</h3>
          <p>I track whether the feature helps in numbers: who uses it, whether it resolves what they came for, and how good the outputs are, checked by both humans and automated grading.</p>
          <p class="technical">In technical terms: Production trace analysis, LLM-as-judge scoring validated against human grading, quality and usage metrics per release.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="ledger-bottom"></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="stack">
    <div class="section-label">
      <span class="section-label__num">02</span>
      <h2>Stack</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="stack-list">
      <div class="stack-row">
        <span class="stack-row__label">Languages</span>
        <p>TypeScript and Node.js, Python, SQL and Postgres. React and Next.js when the work needs it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stack-row">
        <span class="stack-row__label">LLM engineering</span>
        <p>agent and tool design, RAG pipelines, structured outputs, streaming, runtime guardrails, human-in-the-loop design, multi-tenant isolation.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stack-row">
        <span class="stack-row__label">Evaluation</span>
        <p>error analysis on production traces, failure taxonomies, LLM-as-judge validated against human grading, CI suites from production errors, Langfuse.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stack-row">
        <span class="stack-row__label">Infra</span>
        <p>Cloudflare Workers, Docker, Redis, Modal, FastAPI, GitHub Actions.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stack-bottom"></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="fit">
    <div class="section-label">
      <span class="section-label__num">03</span>
      <h2>Fit</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="fit-table">
      <div class="fit-pair fit-pair--head">
        <span class="fit-pair__aspect"></span>
        <span class="fit-col-head">Good fit</span>
        <span class="fit-col-head fit-col-head--less">Less of a fit</span>
      </div>
      <div class="fit-pair">
        <span class="fit-pair__aspect">Ownership</span>
        <p>Deciding what to build: talking to users, choosing the problems worth solving, and answering for the results.</p>
        <p class="fit-less">Work that arrives as pre-scoped tickets, with the problems decided elsewhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fit-pair">
        <span class="fit-pair__aspect">Scope</span>
        <p>Owning a whole outcome, from frontend through backend to the AI, and working across teams to ship it.</p>
        <p class="fit-less">Roles boxed into a single layer, like frontend-only or backend-only.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fit-pair">
        <span class="fit-pair__aspect">Product</span>
        <p>Products where AI is the core, or a feature users have come to depend on.</p>
        <p class="fit-less">Products with no user-facing AI, or where AI only helps write the code.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fit-pair">
        <span class="fit-pair__aspect">AI work</span>
        <p>Applied AI: products built on foundation models, with quality measured on every change.</p>
        <p class="fit-less">ML research or model training.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="fit-bottom"></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="case-studies">
    <div class="section-label">
      <span class="section-label__num">04</span>
      <h2>Case studies</h2>
    </div>
    {studies.map((study) => <CaseStudyCard study={study} />)}
  </section>

  <section id="writing">
    <div class="section-label">
      <span class="section-label__num">05</span>
      <h2>Writing</h2>
    </div>
    <ul>
      {posts.map((post) => (
        <li><a href={`/blog/${post.data.permalink ?? post.id}/`}>{post.data.title}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="background">
    <div class="section-label">
      <span class="section-label__num">06</span>
      <h2>Background</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="resume">
      <p class="resume-sublabel">Experience</p>
      <details class="resume-entry">
        <summary>
          <span class="resume-entry__row">
            <span class="resume-entry__title"><span class="resume-entry__company">ChromaWay</span> <span class="resume-entry__role">Senior Software Engineer / Tech Lead, Platform &amp; Distributed Systems</span></span>
            <span class="resume-entry__years">Stockholm, 2021 to 2026</span>
          </span>
        </summary>
        <ul>
          <li>Backend tech lead on Chromia, a relational Layer 1 blockchain platform; led teams of up to 10 developers and reviewed architecture and implementation across the platform.</li>
          <li>Took several partner projects from zero to one: early conversations with the partner company, a first version built from scratch, then iteration on their feedback.</li>
          <li>Built the blockchain backend for Mines of Dalarnia with one colleague: economics and items for a game that processed over 1M real-money transactions and peaked as the second most used app on Binance Chain.</li>
          <li>Owned the native staking system (1,000+ users). Found and fixed a critical bug causing exponential data growth; the fix resolved a network-wide performance issue.</li>
          <li>Built EVM bridging to Ethereum-family chains, NFC garment authentication for a fashion company, and on-chain crowdfunding and real-estate tokenization platforms handling real financial transactions.</li>
          <li>First real consumer of the platform's on-chain AI services: built AI inference and RAG demos on it and fed bugs and improvements back to the core team.</li>
          <li>Chromia tech expert embedded with the documentation and onboarding team: shaped user-test strategy and questionnaire structure with the team lead, running tests asynchronously through Lyssna.</li>
        </ul>
      </details>
      <details class="resume-entry">
        <summary>
          <span class="resume-entry__row">
            <span class="resume-entry__title"><span class="resume-entry__company">SveaSync</span> <span class="resume-entry__role">Co-founder and technical lead</span></span>
            <span class="resume-entry__years">2026</span>
          </span>
        </summary>
        <p>The full story, decisions, and architecture are in the <a href="/case-studies/sveasync/">case study</a>.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="resume-entry">
        <summary>
          <span class="resume-entry__row">
            <span class="resume-entry__title"><span class="resume-entry__company">Senseworks</span> <span class="resume-entry__role">Frontend Developer (part-time)</span></span>
            <span class="resume-entry__years">2020 to 2021</span>
          </span>
        </summary>
        <p>B2B SaaS for auditors and accountants. Data visualization in React and TypeScript: tables, graphs, REST API integration, Redux Toolkit.</p>
      </details>
      <p class="resume-sublabel">Education</p>
      <div class="resume-entry resume-entry--static">
        <span class="resume-entry__row">
          <span class="resume-entry__title"><span class="resume-entry__company">Umeå University</span> <span class="resume-entry__role">MSc in Engineering programme, Interaction Technology and Design</span></span>
          <span class="resume-entry__years">2016 to 2021</span>
        </span>
        <p>Five-year programme spanning programming, algorithms, statistics, human-computer interaction, human-centred AI, and UX evaluation and research. Exchange semester at Northern Arizona University, fall 2019.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="resume-bottom"></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <aside class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
    <p class="toc__head">On this page</p>
    <a href="#what-i-do"><span class="toc__num">01</span> What I do</a>
    <a href="#stack"><span class="toc__num">02</span> Stack</a>
    <a href="#fit"><span class="toc__num">03</span> Fit</a>
    <a href="#case-studies"><span class="toc__num">04</span> Case studies</a>
    <div class="toc__sub">
      {studies.map((study) => (
        <a href={`#cs-${study.id}`}>{study.data.title.split(':')[0]}</a>
      ))}
    </div>
    <a href="#writing"><span class="toc__num">05</span> Writing</a>
    <a href="#background"><span class="toc__num">06</span> Background</a>
  </aside>
</Base>
